  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to represent the function h as a normalized sum of shifted bump functions, specifically using Weibull functions that allow parameterization of smoothness and width. The optimization strategy involves:\n1. Using multi-scale Gaussian process regression to propose improvements\n2. Maintaining a set of \"bumps\" (features) with fixed width but varying positions and amplitudes\n3. Ensuring global structure through gradient-based refinement\n4. Minimizing maximum overlap through iterative spatial reweighting\n\nThis approach was chosen because:\n- Gaussian processes provide a structure for the function h that naturally captures the desired overlap-minimizing shape\n- The parametric representation reduces dimensionality relative to grid optimization\n- The iterative refinement balances exploitation and exploration\n- The bounded control ensures solutions remain valid\n\nLimitations include:\n- Dependence on initial parameter selection\n- Potential sensitivity to correlation calculation precision\n- Fixed width may limit discovery of optimal shape\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: h(x) = \u03bb * \u2211_{i=1}^\u2075 exp(-(x - \u03bc_i)^2/\u03c3\u00b2 / (1 + \u03c4 exp(-(\u03bc_i^2 + something))\n- Where: \u03bb = 1/\u222bh(x)dx (normalized), \u03bc_i positions, \u03c4 sharpness parameter\n- Objective: minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints: h(x) \u2208 [0,1], \u222bh(x)dx=1\n- Parameters: 15 variables (5 bumps \u00d7 3 parameters each)\n- Justification: The form ensures smoothness (assisted by Weibull), maintains boundedness automatically due to exponential decay, and separable optimization\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validate parent solution (if available) using scipy.optimize + numpy\n2. Initialize: Generate 5 bump centers uniformly in [0,2], set \u03bc_i, \u03c4 (fixed), \u03b7_i (heights)\n3. Optimization loop:\n   a. Compute candidate h(x) = \u03b7_i * exp(-(x - \u03bc_i)^2/\u03c3\u00b2) for all i\n   b. Normalize: \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   c. Compute clipped h(x) \u2208 [0,1]\n   d. Calculate C5 = max_\u03ba \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   e. For M iterations:\n      i. Sample new centers \u03bc_candidate with Gaussian noise\n      ii. Compute gradient of C5 with respect to parameters\n      iii. Use SLSQP with box constraints (\u03bc_i \u2208 [0,2], \u03b7_i > 0)\n      iv. Repair: Clip values to [0,1] & rebalance integrals\n\n4. Stage 2: Hairpin adjustment\n   a. For \u03bc_i near boundary (0.2 or 1.8), invert order\n   b. Vary width \u03c3 in [0.3,0.7] during refinement\n   c. Adaptive step size: reduce optimization steps if constraints tighten\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Primary: \u03c4 = 1.5 (fixed sharpness), \u03b7_i initial range = [0.7,0.9]\n- Fallback: If plateau found, vary \u03c4 by \u00b120%\n- Neighborhood: Apply Simpson's rule for integrals rather than trapezoidal for precision\n\nTime budget & robustness:\n- Stage 1: 300s grid optimization (n=5000 points)\n- Stage 2: 500s gradient refinement\n- Check for NaN values in correlations\n- Fallback solution if timeout: Semi-symmetric bump arrangement\n\nValidation contract:\n- Compute C5 using scipy.correlate with mode='full' and scaling\n- Verify \u222bh(x)dx=1 and h(x) \u2208 [0,1]\n- Return updated parent solution if improvement found",
